# Copy to deploy/.env and set the value for THIS host and THIS environment.
#
# deploy/.env is gitignored, so it does not travel with a clone or a pull — a
# fresh checkout has no copy of it, and `docker compose -f deploy/…` reads
# `.env` from the directory it is *run in*, not from deploy/. Both of those
# together mean a deploy that looks completely normal can silently start the
# receiver with no trusted proxy at all. Always pass --env-file:
#
#   docker compose --env-file deploy/.env -f deploy/compose.nonprod.yml up -d --build
#
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS is the npm-attachment container's address on this site's
# network, and it changes whenever npm-attachment is recreated:
#
#   docker inspect -f \
#     '{{range $n, $c := .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{$n}} {{$c.IPAddress}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}' \
#     npm-attachment
#
# Check it took: `docker logs henley-website-forms-<env> | head` prints the
# addresses it will believe, or warns that it will believe none of them.
TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=
